WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.928
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.494 --> 00:00:10.910
QUENTIN HARDY: I'd
like to welcome

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.620
our special guests,
Renee Murphy,

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.440
Principal Analyst for Security
and Risk at Forrester,

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.690
and Ben McCormack, Vice
President of Operations

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:18.500
at Evernote.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.270
Renee, can you tell
us a little bit

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:23.390
about your work at Forrester
on security in the enterprise

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:24.930
and how Cloud figures into that?

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:26.680
RENEE MURPHY: Yeah,
so I work at Forrester

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:28.580
as a principal analyst
in governance, risk,

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:29.430
and compliance.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:32.299
So I work with chief
information security officers

00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:35.079
on the risk question
about the cloud.

00:00:35.079 --> 00:00:36.620
And from a security
perspective, I'll

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:39.036
say that that's usually the
very first question they have.

00:00:39.036 --> 00:00:40.070
How secure is the cloud?

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:44.125
Are they going to be
responsible for my problems?

00:00:44.125 --> 00:00:45.500
The first thing
you can try to do

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:49.400
is tell them, listen, take
any Cloud best practice,

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.920
go into your own data center,
and see if you'll pass that.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:52.790
And they'll come
back and tell me,

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:54.164
there's no way we can do this.

00:00:54.164 --> 00:00:54.830
I'm like, right.

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:55.700
Well then--

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.000
QUENTIN HARDY: Not much
left to talk about.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:57.380
RENEE MURPHY: Right?

00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:59.010
It's like, someone
else can it better.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:00.360
You might want to do it.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.440
So I think that's how they
first come to the question.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:05.104
Like Like, what am
I really giving up?

00:01:05.104 --> 00:01:06.770
And I say, you're not
giving up anything

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:09.894
except the spinning
disk in your space.

00:01:09.894 --> 00:01:11.060
That's all you're giving up.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:12.726
You're actually
enhancing your security.

00:01:12.726 --> 00:01:14.294
You're enhancing
your availability.

00:01:14.294 --> 00:01:15.710
What makes Cloud
more effective is

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:18.740
you can hire people
I can't hire.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:21.240
You can have huge data
centers that I can't have.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:22.120
QUENTIN HARDY: Me
being all of Google.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:22.520
RENEE MURPHY: All of Google.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:23.240
QUENTIN HARDY: OK.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:24.110
RENEE MURPHY: You can do that.

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:24.630
Right?

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:26.060
You can do it in a way I can't.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.239
And listen, the more of
us that go to the cloud,

00:01:28.239 --> 00:01:29.780
the better you're
going to get at it.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.010
And the better you
get at it, the better

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:33.020
I'm going to be at it.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:35.630
And I think at the
end of the day,

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:37.790
if you're in the business
of doing anything

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:39.560
other than managing
data centers,

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.550
then why are you
managing the data center?

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:43.216
QUENTIN HARDY: Ben,
let me bring you in.

00:01:43.216 --> 00:01:45.980
Last year, you did migrate
your data centers over

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:46.889
to Google Cloud.

00:01:46.889 --> 00:01:48.680
Tell us a little bit
about that experience.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.170
Does the security
side of that mirror

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:52.550
what Rene is talking about?

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:55.310
Or did you have other
interests and concerns?

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:57.200
BEN MCCORMACK: I'd
say broadly, yes.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.200
For us, it was about
allowing our teams

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.080
to focus on what was important
to our end customers.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:05.240
We've got hundreds
of millions of users

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.060
who we are helping collect
information, retrieve

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:10.699
information, and help
them be more efficient.

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:12.920
Being an expert in running
a physical data center

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:14.570
doesn't directly support that.

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:17.992
So for us, it was about
allowing our technical teams--

00:02:17.992 --> 00:02:18.700
and you're right.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.470
You move to Cloud, you do not
get rid of your technical team.

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:21.860
RENEE MURPHY: Nope.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:22.510
BEN MCCORMACK: Or
if you do, you're

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:25.000
going to realize it's a
mistake really quickly.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.380
There are things you are
doing in the physical data

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.990
center that will be
replaced in the cloud.

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:33.590
You need to become an expert
at the cloud provider's control

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:35.280
plane, et cetera, et cetera.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.070
But for us, it
was about focusing

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:39.320
our company and
our staff on things

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:42.140
that were important
to our users and also

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:44.030
allowing our development
teams and software

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.650
engineers to move a lot faster.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:48.980
So for us, the rate
of change and the rate

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:52.130
of innovation in the
cloud space is way faster

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:53.472
than we can ever achieve.

00:02:53.472 --> 00:02:55.430
QUENTIN HARDY: That's
the proposition in Cloud,

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:58.340
that it automates a lot
of the [INAUDIBLE] work.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:02:59.790
You don't pull cable anymore.

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:02.300
You don't destroy
hard drive anymore.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:05.660
You outsource all that
to another provider

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:07.550
in a large centralized area.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.270
And developers can move faster.

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:13.040
If they have better
tools, they can scale.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.800
Ultimately, security questions
are about having your business

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:17.257
relationships threatened.

00:03:17.257 --> 00:03:19.340
And you must have had
security questions going in.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:20.173
What were they like?

00:03:20.173 --> 00:03:21.840
And how were they
best addressed?

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.090
BEN MCCORMACK: So for us,
I think it was twofold.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:26.360
We initially started with
our standard security

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:27.560
questionnaires.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:29.180
We went through those.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:31.130
And then what our
security team actually did

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:34.880
was paused a couple of months
in and actually started

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:38.330
risk-modeling or threat-modeling
what were the risks that we

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:39.890
were looking at in Cloud.

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:42.650
The risk model is not,
is the cloud provider

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:44.390
going to lose a hard drive?

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:47.510
The risk model is what happens
if you lose your, effectively,

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:50.450
your root password in
your Cloud account.

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:53.870
So we went through our
standard process first.

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:56.260
We learned from that
in the first cycle.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:58.140
And then we updated
our threat modeling.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.951
And then reasked the questions
to Google and reengaged.

00:04:00.951 --> 00:04:02.450
And I think the
other thing, we just

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:04.760
went through a
period of learning

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:06.830
that you have to--
to go to Cloud means

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:08.570
you've got to trust somebody.

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:11.000
QUENTIN HARDY: Is there
some kind of corollary

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:12.380
where security is concerned?

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:14.450
Are the security teams
working in different ways

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:15.556
on more valuable stuff?

00:04:15.556 --> 00:04:17.430
BEN MCCORMACK: It's
broadly exactly the same.

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:19.800
So the security team
and the legal teams

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:23.390
have still got to verify
all the certifications

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:24.260
with the provider.

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:26.900
You've got to go through
the security certifications,

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:28.100
the questionnaires.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:29.640
None of that changes.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.660
But what the security team is
not having to do is audit--

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:35.510
we use this example-- physical
hard drive destruction.

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:37.610
They're not having to
audit physical hard drive

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:39.830
destruction logs every quarter.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:43.010
And for us, the question becomes
if they're not doing that,

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:45.170
what can they be
spending their time on?

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:48.560
They can spend more time on
securing the application, more

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:51.650
time helping our developers
write more secure code,

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:53.750
and more time
chasing people that

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:55.880
are trying to hack
our application,

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.550
not trying to hack our
physical data centers.

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:01.740
So for us, I think on the
security side as well,

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:04.160
it allows them to
move up the stack

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:05.750
and focus on things
that are more

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:07.740
important to our customers.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.420
And ultimately, that's
what it's about.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:11.510
It's about your customers.

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:14.200
It's not about the running
physical data centers.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:15.270
That's how we saw it.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:17.180
QUENTIN HARDY: And
you're offering your team

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:19.670
more interesting and
challenging work.

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:20.870
It is a new landscape.

00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:23.120
What do you wish
you'd known beforehand

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:25.100
about security specifically?

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:27.230
BEN MCCORMACK: For us,
there hasn't really been--

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:31.210
there's been no big surprises
on the security side.

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:33.080
We did all of our due
diligence upfront.

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:35.600
Our security team and our
legal team spent a lot of time

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:37.820
with the Google
teams understanding

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:40.490
the nuances of Cloud, what
did it provide, what didn't it

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:41.150
provide.

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:44.220
The big surprise for us has
been how quickly our software

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:47.240
engineers embraced the cloud.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:49.740
And in some ways, we're
having to play catch-up.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:51.614
QUENTIN HARDY: It's
interesting you say this,

00:05:51.614 --> 00:05:54.290
because you're telling me that
the IT teams stay in place.

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:56.030
Security stays in place.

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:57.710
They're involved
in different work,

00:05:57.710 --> 00:05:59.870
so there's some change
management involved.

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:02.769
And you see a competitive
differentiator

00:06:02.769 --> 00:06:04.310
for the enterprise
in going to Cloud.

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:05.540
What was that about?

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:07.998
BEN MCCORMACK: I think you
gotta look at the macro picture.

00:06:07.998 --> 00:06:09.560
We're losing the battle.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:11.900
Breaches are getting
larger and larger.

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:14.390
Threat actors are getting
more and more sophisticated.

00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:16.700
And as an industry, I'm
not sure we're actually

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:18.570
winning that battle.

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:23.270
But if you can focus less on
some of the security layers

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:24.860
and allow your
cloud provider, who

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:28.276
has scale, who can apply
automation, who can apply,

00:06:28.276 --> 00:06:29.900
we hope in the future,
machine learning

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:33.230
to some of those things, it
frees up security professionals

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:36.334
to focus on our
applications' security.

00:06:36.334 --> 00:06:38.000
And I think it's one
of the ways that we

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:40.970
may start stemming
the tide of losing

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:43.790
the battle against hacks.

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:46.190
QUENTIN HARDY: And Renee,
you speak to many businesses.

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:48.740
How does security figure
in for most of them

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:51.989
as becoming some sort of
competitive differentiator?

00:06:51.989 --> 00:06:53.780
RENEE MURPHY: What
we're trying to manage--

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:55.340
businesses, when
they talk to me,

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:57.300
they're trying to manage
reputational risk.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:57.920
Right?

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:00.020
And so what do you end
up with after a breach?

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:01.620
You have damaged reputation.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:02.120
Right?

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:03.895
And so everybody's
trying to control that.

00:07:03.895 --> 00:07:06.020
And actually, it's how they
get funding these days.

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:08.394
We don't talk about threaten
and vulnerabilities anymore.

00:07:08.394 --> 00:07:11.900
We talk about the impact to
the business's reputation.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:14.660
Are we going to be labeled
another company who can't seem

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:15.890
to keep their data together?

00:07:15.890 --> 00:07:17.510
QUENTIN HARDY: Will
there be a massive fine?

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:19.160
RENEE MURPHY: And will
there be a massive fine?

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:20.659
Yeah, that's not
even funny anymore.

00:07:20.659 --> 00:07:22.170
So we can't ignore it.

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:24.560
So I think from a
differentiator perspective

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:27.440
that if you are spending
your time, like Ben said,

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:30.710
working on other things,
securing your application,

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:33.140
making better code, having
security deployed somewhere

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:34.940
else so that they
can be meaningful,

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:38.990
I think that's all part
of being a differentiator,

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:40.280
a competitive differentiator.

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:41.630
QUENTIN HARDY: And of
course, the customers

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:44.100
are also thinking, how is
this going to change my life?

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:46.280
And so for a security
engineer, when

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:50.570
you talk about cloud security
and how pervasive or full stack

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:53.660
or depth of hardware,
software, security integration,

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:57.260
whatever, what are they
thinking about what

00:07:57.260 --> 00:08:01.039
they're going to do next if that
part of their job goes away?

00:08:01.039 --> 00:08:01.830
RENEE MURPHY: Yeah.

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:02.480
See, that's it.

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:04.430
We spend a lot of
time telling them,

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:06.680
we need to figure out
a way to get you out

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:09.950
of the day-to-day
stuff of security,

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:12.824
looking at patching,
looking at incidences,

00:08:12.824 --> 00:08:14.990
trying to figure out if the
threat and vulnerability

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:17.700
management system is
giving us false positives.

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:21.110
So for me, it's all about
managing that third party,

00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:22.760
having a partnership,
understanding

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:25.350
who's responsible for what,
negotiating that ahead of time.

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:25.850
Right?

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:27.920
In a breach, who
notifies who of what?

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:31.070
In a security incident, how are
we going to communicate that?

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:32.900
Because you may be
communicating to me.

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:35.220
But I need to communicate
to an entire organization.

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:37.400
So we need to be
in sync with that.

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:39.289
We need to negotiate
change management.

00:08:39.289 --> 00:08:41.000
We need to negotiate
incident management.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.220
If I'm in a heavily
regulated industry

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:45.740
and you're making infrastructure
changes, I have to know.

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:47.640
QUENTIN HARDY: So
transparency is available.

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:48.205
Exploit it.

00:08:48.205 --> 00:08:48.830
Use it as much.

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:50.300
RENEE MURPHY: Yes,
absolutely do it.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:52.216
And if you're in a heavily
regulated industry,

00:08:52.216 --> 00:08:53.930
have one conversation
with the regulators

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:57.770
before you go down this road to
understand their expectations.

00:08:57.770 --> 00:08:59.930
And if you have internal
audit, have them involved.

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:02.150
BEN MCCORMACK: The
landscape has changed.

00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:05.270
And I think the next generation
of IT professionals, security

00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:08.360
professionals, need
to be cloud-ready.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:12.080
They need to be less thinking
about how do I secure a router

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:14.670
and more thinking about how
do I secure my Cloud account,

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:18.300
how do I secure my keys, and
continue on that journey.

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:21.800
So I think it's a journey
up the stack for all of us.

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:23.420
When I started in
the industry, we

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:25.130
used to argue about
what physical network

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:26.185
card to put in a server.

00:09:26.185 --> 00:09:27.020
RENEE MURPHY: [LAUGHS] Yeah.

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:27.350
BEN MCCORMACK: We did.

00:09:27.350 --> 00:09:28.141
It was an argument.

00:09:28.141 --> 00:09:31.730
It took you a month of time to
discuss what version of card

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:32.990
you would put in.

00:09:32.990 --> 00:09:34.550
We're constantly
moving up the stack.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:35.972
And it's only going to continue.

00:09:35.972 --> 00:09:37.680
And it's going to move
faster and faster.

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:39.138
QUENTIN HARDY: And
Ben, what do you

00:09:39.138 --> 00:09:41.885
see your security teams working
on a year or two from now?

00:09:41.885 --> 00:09:43.760
BEN MCCORMACK: So what
we're trying to assess

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:45.950
is how far can we
go down the route

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:50.930
of serverless architectures, not
worrying about infrastructure,

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:53.660
continuing to obstruct
further and further away

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:55.590
from the underlying
infrastructure.

00:09:55.590 --> 00:09:57.404
We're not sure it's
quite there yet.

00:09:57.404 --> 00:09:59.195
But we know it's coming
very, very quickly.

00:09:59.195 --> 00:10:00.894
And we want to be
ready to embrace that.

00:10:00.894 --> 00:10:03.560
QUENTIN HARDY: A more integrated
and automated stack, basically.

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:04.851
BEN MCCORMACK: Basically, yeah.

00:10:04.851 --> 00:10:06.440
Embracing things
like Cloud Functions,

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:10.140
and embracing those
higher value services.

00:10:10.140 --> 00:10:11.000
Renee said it.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:13.520
Why worry about
securing the container?

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:15.310
Worry about securing the code.

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:17.370
Worry about writing secure code.

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:20.249
So for us, it's a
continual abstraction.

00:10:20.249 --> 00:10:21.290
It's never going to stop.

00:10:21.290 --> 00:10:22.707
And it's only going
to get faster.

00:10:22.707 --> 00:10:24.956
QUENTIN HARDY: What does the
future hold for security?

00:10:24.956 --> 00:10:26.310
What do you see happening next?

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:28.460
What do you think businesses
should be thinking about?

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:29.300
RENEE MURPHY: Well,
I think businesses

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:31.877
should be thinking about getting
comfortable with the idea

00:10:31.877 --> 00:10:33.710
that you don't need to
manage this yourself.

00:10:33.710 --> 00:10:35.570
I think we should
see this as fully

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:37.940
outsourced from a
security perspective,

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:39.830
between the managed
security service

00:10:39.830 --> 00:10:41.690
providers or cloud providers.

00:10:41.690 --> 00:10:44.660
I think we, we as
organizations, should get out

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:45.380
of that business.

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:47.210
We shouldn't be in the
business of securing data.

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:49.940
We should have companies that
are very good at securing data.

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:52.460
We should share
that risk with them.

00:10:52.460 --> 00:10:55.250
We should do that because
you hired all the best

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:57.920
people that I can't afford.

00:10:57.920 --> 00:10:58.430
Right?

00:10:58.430 --> 00:10:59.790
You hired all the best
people that I can't afford.

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:00.740
BEN MCCORMACK:
She's got a point.

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:03.530
RENEE MURPHY: So I may as well
have you manage that for me.

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:06.320
Listen, if I'm in the
business of doing research,

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:08.830
should I be in the business
of running a data center?

00:11:08.830 --> 00:11:09.710
Again, no.

00:11:09.710 --> 00:11:11.150
No, I shouldn't be.

00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:11.690
Right?

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:13.161
Other people are better at it.

00:11:13.161 --> 00:11:14.660
QUENTIN HARDY: Talk
to the provider.

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:16.970
Get a roadmap for what's
going to get automated next,

00:11:16.970 --> 00:11:19.310
and think about the
value-added security layer

00:11:19.310 --> 00:11:21.147
you might put on top of that.

00:11:21.147 --> 00:11:21.980
That's great advice.

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:25.030
[MUSIC PLAYING]

